  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">When KROSER Laptop Briefcase (Up to 17.3") wins</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Pick KROSER Laptop Briefcase (Up to 17.3") if...</h2>
    <p>The laptop is the reason you carry a bag at all. Briefcase carries everything: laptop, charger, notebook, headphones, water bottle. If you'd otherwise need to carry two things (laptop bag + tote), the briefcase consolidates.</p><p>You commute by transit or walk regularly. Shoulder strap makes hands-free carry practical. Slim sleeves without a strap require a second bag.</p><p>You carry a 17-inch laptop. Slim sleeves at that size are rare and often don't accommodate the machine plus a modest charger.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">When Tomtoc 360 Slim Sleeve wins</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Pick Tomtoc 360 Slim Sleeve if...</h2>
    <p>You already carry a backpack for other reasons (gym, groceries, kids' stuff) and just need padding for the laptop inside it. Adding a slim sleeve is the right move; buying a second laptop bag isn't.</p><p>You value low weight and minimalist carry. The tomtoc 360 adds maybe 200g to whatever bag you already have. The KROSER adds a whole bag.</p><p>You use the laptop mostly at fixed desks (home, office) and only carry it occasionally. A slim sleeve stays in the closet between trips. A briefcase demands to be used.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">The verdict</p>
    <p>Backpack person: buy the tomtoc sleeve. It's the correct add-on to a bag you already carry.</p><p>Briefcase person, transit commuter, or 17-inch laptop owner: buy the KROSER. Full-size carry bag is what you actually need.</p><p>Both is fine too. The sleeve inside the briefcase adds redundant protection on rough travel days.</p>
  </section>

